From f423351bc185f234e539ae60dd430741f38e4e07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Guilhem Bichot <guilhem.bichot@contributor>

Date: Wed, 17 Sep 2025 10:04:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [plan_cache][wrong result] after enable
 plan_cache, a query including a where subclause like "tinyblob_col IS NOT
 UNKNOWN AND int_col NOT BETWEEN ? AND ?" return the result set with wrong
 sort

BUGFIX after enable plan_cache, In the scenario where subqueries are nested and subqueries of the year type are queried, for debug pkg: mysqld will crash; for release pkg:the result is incorrect when the plan_cache feature is used for the second time.

The two bugs have the same general cause: MRR (multi-range-read) vs
plan cache.

Background: when we cache a plan and this plan used a range access
path, we do not cache this access path (a rather complex object
containing range-optimizer-specific data structures which are possibly
dependent on the values of the prepared statement's parameters); we
rather cache the type of this path and the used index; later when we
apply this cached plan, we regenerate a range access path from
the (type,index) information. This usually works well. But sometimes
the regeneration produces a different range access path (this can
happen because values of parameters are very different, or due to more
obscure implementation details), and we check for a few unacceptable
differences, and may then ditch the cached plan.

BUGFIX :

- the original range access plan is produced by check_quick_select(),
without the HA_MRR_SORTED flag ; then test_if_skip_order() adds this
flag. When we regenerate the plan, we only call test_quick_select(),
so do not get the flag. As a result, data is not sorted.
- fix: remember if the original plan had HA_MRR_SORTED, and if yes,
add it to the new plan.

BUGFIX :
- it is actually not a bug, as it uses a subquery which has
non-deterministic results (which has one row due to aggregation, and
thus a choice to make for which value to return for the non-aggregated
column). So it is perfectly ok if the second execution does not return
the same data as the first execution, because it has chosen a
different plan for example.
- however, closer examination to find out why the applied plan is
different from the original plan (an annoying fact: after all, this is
plan cache!), revealed a problem. When we regenerate the range access
path, we pass skip_records_in_range=true to test_quick_select(). This
is done for performance reasons, however it implies that the estimated
number of records is different (it is 1 instead of ~4000, see "Since
records_in_range has not been called" in
handler::multi_range_read_info_const()), and so is the buffer size
used by Disk-Sweep MRR (600 bytes instead of ~29000). This is the
reason why, in our testcase, output data is organized differently and
we get a different choice of value for the random row ; and in
general, if the new buffer is much smaller than the old one, it can be
inefficient.
- fix: we remember the buffer's size of the original plan, and if the
new buffer's size is smaller, we increase it. For testing, we add the
relevant subquery as a standalone query, this is enough.

For these two bugs, we introduce index_range_scan_props in
Exec_context. It hold many range access path details, including those
which used to be lost when we do plan caching:
- 'reverse' (fixed in a previous bugfix)
- 'mrr_flags' (for sorting)
- 'mrr_buff_size'
Additionally, for other details stored in this struct, we check if
they are different in the regenerated range access path, and if so we
invalidate the cached plan ; this is defensive programming.

With this last check, it was discovered that sometimes, the old plan
has no HA_MRR_SORTED and the regenerated plan has it (which is the
contrary of BUGFIX). It means that the original range access
was created without consideration of ORDER BY (see 'order_direction'
usage in check_quick_select()); it can happen if the caller was
get_quick_record_count(). So, we now do the same when regenerating the
plan.

Finally, an assert() is changed to "if ... return error", so that it
does something in release builds too.

In the mtr test for dstore: OPTIMIZE TABLE sometimes fails with a
deadlock error, this is due to the recent enablement of OPTIMIZE TABLE
in dstore which converts it to ALTER TABLE (before that, dstore simply
skipped this statement), it is a known bug. I was advised to disable
the auto-updating of histogram statistics to work around this. And
this trick also helps with an old problem, which was that the plan
reported by explain_distinct_ranges was not stable when using
dstore (sometimes range access, other times index scan).
